[R0225] year=2025 | venue=International Conference on Electronics, AI, and Computing: Innovating for a Sustainable and Connected Future, EAIC 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: VAP-6: A Benchmarking Framework on Vulnerability Assessment and Penetration Testing for Language Models
KEYWORDS: Benchmark; Cybersecurity; LLM; Local LLM; Open-Source LLM; Penetration Testing; Small Language Models; VAPT; Vulnerability Assessment; Artificial intelligence; Computational efficiency; Computational linguistics; Cybersecurity; Open systems; Benchmark; Cyber security; Language model; Large language model; Local large language model; Open-source; Open-source large language model; Penetration testing; Small language model; Vulnerability assessment and penetration testing; Vulnerability assessments; Benchmarking
ABSTRACT: The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into cybersecurity operations, particularly Vulnerability Assessment and Penetration Testing (VAPT), has shown significant promise. However, there remains a scarcity of comprehensive benchmarks for evaluating LLMs in the VAPT domain, especially for small, open-source models suitable for local deployment. This paper introduces VAP-6, a novel benchmark comprising six distinct datasets designed to evaluate LLM capabilities across crucial VAPT knowledge domains: Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVE) and Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE) identification, Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) prediction, scenario-based reasoning aligned with Certified Ethical Hacker (CEH) v12 and CompTIA PenTest+ PT0-002 certification exams, VAPT tools proficiency, and CVE-to-Metasploit module mapping. We introduce the VAP-6 methodology, encompassing dataset creation from authoritative sources like CVE and CWE MITRE, Exploit DB and Github with refinement through ChatGPT and manual verification. The benchmark was applied to evaluate selected open-source LLMs with parameters ranging from 2 to 3 billion (Qwen 2.5, Gemma2, Llama 3.2), employing Q4 quantization to ensure local computational efficiency via Ollama. This research establishes a standardized framework for benchmarking and comparing such LLMs, facilitating the development of more robust, private, and computationally efficient AI tools for VAPT professionals. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0226] year=2026 | venue=Conference Proceedings - IEEE SOUTHEASTCON | type=Conference paper
TITLE: From Social Engineering to Financial Fraud: A Multi-Agent LLM Approach to Vishing Detection
KEYWORDS: cybersecurity; explainable AI; large language models; multiagent systems; Vishing detection; Behavioral research; Computational linguistics; Computer crime; Crime; Deep learning; Error detection; Intelligent agents; Learning systems; Social sciences computing; Speech recognition; Cyber security; Explainable AI; Financial fraud; Language model; Large language model; Model-based OPC; Multi agent; Phishing detections; Social engineering; Vishing detection; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Voice phishing (vishing) attacks have become increasingly sophisticated, driven by large-scale call centers, social engineering scripts, and AI-generated synthetic voices, posing serious risks to individuals and organizations. Existing vishing detection approaches predominantly rely on monolithic machine learning or deep learning models that offer limited transparency and struggle to capture the multi-dimensional nature of fraudulent voice interactions. This paper presents a novel, web-based vishing detection platform (VishGuard) powered by a large language model (LLM)-based multi-agent system that provides accurate, explainable, and real-time detection across voice calls, emails, and text messages. The proposed architecture decomposes the detection task into six specialized agents, each focusing on a distinct dimension of fraudulent behavior, including product and service deception, linguistic threat intelligence, social engineering tactics, financial fraud indicators, and compliance and legal violations. A central evaluator aggregates evidence generated by these agents to produce a calibrated scam likelihood score and an interpretable rationale. The analytical agents are implemented using the OpenAI GPT-4o Mini model, while voice inputs are processed through automatic speech recognition (ASR) using the Google Gemini 2.5 Flash model. To evaluate the system, a balanced dataset of 3,000 records was constructed, consisting of safe and malicious voice messages and call transcripts. The dataset was primarily curated by domain experts, augmented with synthetically generated samples using GPT-5 and Claude Sonnet 4.5, and further expanded through paraphrasing, Synonym replacement, and audio noise injection. Experimental results demonstrate strong performance, achieving 97.25% accuracy, 99.48% precision, 95% recall, and 97.19% F1-score, while maintaining a low false-positive rate of 0.5%. These results indicate that the proposed multi-agent approach effectively balances detection sensitivity with usability and trust. Overall, this work advances vishing defense by introducing an explainable, modular, and deployment-ready AI system that addresses key limitations of existing ML-, DL-, and LLM-based detection methods. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0227] year=2026 | venue=International Conference on Electrical, Computer, and Energy Technologies, ICECET 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: GenAI-Driven Autonomous DevSecOps Pipelines for Secure Financial Infrastructure
KEYWORDS: CI/CD automation; cybersecurity analytics; DevSecOps; GenAI; software delivery pipelines; Artificial intelligence; Automation; Finance; Learning systems; Network security; Pipelines; Predictive analytics; Risk management; Software reliability; Telemetering; Continuous integration and continuous delivery automation; Continuous integrations; Cyber security; Cybersecurity analytic; Devsecop; GenAI; High velocity; Security risks; Software delivery pipeline; Software-systems; Decision making
ABSTRACT: Modern financial software systems rely heavily on continuous integration and continuous delivery (CI/CD) pipelines to deploy features, security updates, and infrastructure changes at high velocity. While DevSecOps practices have improved automation in software delivery, most pipeline governance still depends on manual decision-making for handling failures, vulnerabilities, and deployment anomalies. This human dependency introduces operational delays, increases security risk exposure, and limits scalability of software delivery pipelines. This paper proposes a GenAI-driven autonomous DevSecOps orchestration framework designed to enable predictive security analytics and automated control actions in CI/CD pipelines. The framework integrates real-time pipeline telemetry with machine learning models capable of detecting anomalies, forecasting deployment failures, and identifying security risks prior to pipeline completion. A GenAI reasoning layer orchestrates remediation strategies including automated rollbacks, security gating, and adaptive pipeline optimization. Experimental evaluation using representative CI/CD telemetry demonstrates that the proposed system improves failure prediction accuracy, reduces mean time to recovery, and enhances overall deployment reliability in financial software infrastructure. The results indicate that integrating generative AI with DevSecOps orchestration can significantly enhance resilience and security in modern financial technology platforms. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0228] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Smart Internet of Things, SmartIoT 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Improving IoT Cyber Threat Response with Multi-Agent LLMs and Semantic Retrieval
KEYWORDS: iot security; multi-agent llm; threat response; Cybersecurity; Information retrieval; Intelligent agents; Intelligent systems; Iterative methods; Knowledge management; Network security; Query processing; Semantics; Structured Query Language; Context-Aware; Cyber security; Cyber threats; Iot security; Multi agent; Multi-agent llm; Question answering systems; Semantic retrieval; Structured domain knowledge; Threat response; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: The increasing complexity of IoT cybersecurity challenges demands intelligent systems capable of integrating structured domain knowledge with advanced reasoning to deliver accurate and context-aware responses. Traditional LLM-based question-answering systems often produce hallucinated or outdated answers, posing significant risks in IoT environments where vulnerabilities span heterogeneous devices and protocols. To mitigate this, we propose a novel multi-agent framework specifically designed for IoT security applications. This architecture decomposes complex security queries, retrieves evidence from structured repositories such as MITRE ATT&CK for ICS and IoT-specific threat taxonomies, and synthesizes final answers through coordinated reasoning agents. Our demonstration employs a lightweight LLaMA 3.2 model for tasks like intent classification and query parsing, while leveraging Mistral-7B for in-depth reasoning and response generation. These components are integrated with dense-vector and transformer-based retrieval techniques. The framework incorporates iterative feedback loops among agents, allowing for query refinement, evidence validation, and contradiction resolution, ultimately enabling trustworthy and grounded responses for IoT cyber threat detection and mitigation. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0229] year=2024 | venue=Road Materials and Pavement Design | type=Article
TITLE: Effect of superficially modified zinc oxide nanoparticles as an additive on the rheological performance of asphalt binder
KEYWORDS: Asphalt binder; nanomaterials; rheological properties; surface effect; zinc oxide; Additives; Asphalt; Particle size; ZnO nanoparticles; Asphalt binders; Large surface area; Penetration index; Performance; Physical tests; Rheological property; Small particle size; Surface effect; Trimethoxysilane; Zinc oxide nanoparticles; II-VI semiconductors
ABSTRACT: The surface effect that hinders the dispersion of nanomaterials in the asphalt binder due to the large surface area and small particle size should be investigated. Thus, this study evaluates the effect of the incorporation of superficially modified zinc oxide nanoparticles (ZnO M) by 3-(2.3-epoxypropoxy)propyl trimethoxysilane (GPTMS) in the asphalt binder with 50/70 penetration index. The additive was incorporated in contents of 3%, 5%, and 7% in weight of the bind through physical and rheological tests. Penetration tests, softening point, rotational viscosity, performance grade (PG)–before and after rolling thin film oven (RTFO), multiple stress creep and recovery (MSCR), linear amplitude sweep (LAS), and master curve were performed. The addition of ZnO M nanoparticles provided the asphalt binder with more resistance to aging and oxidation in almost all properties analysed. In addition, the ZnO M increased resistance to permanent deformation, temperature field, and the integrity of the asphalt binder, thus increasing its fatigue life. © 2023 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.

[R0230] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE Jordan Conference on Applied Electrical Engineering and Computing Technologies, AEECT 2026 - Proceeding | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Cyber Threat Intelligence Report Generation Using Agentic AI
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Cyber Threat Intelligence; GraphRAG; Large Language Models; RAG; Computation theory; Cybersecurity; Decision making; Knowledge management; Network security; Agentic AI; Cybe threat intelligence; Cyber security; Cyber threats; Graphrag; High quality; Language model; Large language model; RAG; Work-flows; Artificial intelligence
ABSTRACT: Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) reports are instrumental communication tools for keeping decision makers and cybersecurity practitioners informed about the rapidly evolving cyberspace. However, the explosive growth of CTI sources has made ingestion, processing, and analysis for intelligence reporting increasingly difficult and error-prone.To address this, we introduce an end-to-end framework that ingests cybersecurity attack trends and streaming news, augmented with feeds from cybersecurity articles, into an agentic AI workflow. Further enhanced by a GraphRAG database and a vector database, this approach strengthens the capabilities of LLMs. The result is the generation of CTI analysis reports with high-quality and grounded data sources aimed at supporting and enhancing the decision-making process. We evaluate the system using custom grounding and consistency strategy, revealing strong performance when the workflow retrieves directly from RSS evidence and similarly high-quality synthesis when leveraging the knowledge graph. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0231] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of 7th International Conference on Inventive Material Science and Applications, ICIMA 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Comprehensive Web Fuzzer with AI Mitigation and Custom Payload
KEYWORDS: AI-driven fuzzing; OWASP ZAP; penetration testing; Web application security; Industrial management; Network security; Remediation; Websites; AI-driven fuzzing; Intelligent decision-making; OWASP ZAP; Penetration testing; Real- time; Security testing; Testing process; Vulnerability detection; Vulnerability remediations; Web application security; Decision making
ABSTRACT: This paper addresses the challenge of inefficient and manually intensive web application security testing, which often results in delayed vulnerability detection and remediation. The system proposes Cyber Xipher, an AI-driven fuzzing framework that streamlines the penetration testing process through automation and intelligent decision-making. The system integrates Gemini AI for real-time website classification, OWASP ZAP for adaptive payload-based fuzzing, and a large language model (LLM) to generate contextual mitigation reports. Cyber Xipher not only crafts targeted attack payloads based on website context but also automates the reporting and remediation workflow. Through a centralized admin dashboard, it enables tester assignment, monitoring, and oversight functionalities. Experimental validation demonstrates that Cyber Xipher significantly improves test coverage, reduces manual effort, and accelerates vulnerability remediation. The framework is positioned as a strategic tool for enterprise security operations and automated vulnerability management. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0232] year=2026 | venue=ACM Computing Surveys | type=Article
TITLE: Adversarial Machine Learning in IoT Security: A Comprehensive Survey
KEYWORDS: Adversarial machine learning; cybersecurity; generative AI; internet of things; intrusion detection; Adversarial machine learning; Computer crime; Cybersecurity; Ecosystems; Edge computing; Ethical aspects; Generative adversarial networks; Human computer interaction; Learning systems; Network security; Surveying; Comprehensive analysis; Critical approach; Current limitation; Cyber security; Emerging technologies; Generative AI; Intrusion Detection Systems; Intrusion-Detection; Machine-learning; System robustness; Internet of things; Intrusion detection
ABSTRACT: This survey presents a comprehensive analysis of Adversarial Machine Learning (AML) as a critical approach for enhancing the security of Internet of Things (IoT) ecosystems, with a focus on its integration into Intrusion Detection Systems (IDSs). It synthesizes existing research, identifies current limitations, and outlines future directions for effective AML deployment. The survey explores emerging technologies to improve IDS robustness, adaptability, and threat anticipation capabilities. It further addresses the challenges of implementing AML in resource-constrained IoT environments. A structured, multi-phase attack framework inspired by the MITRE ATT&CK model is proposed to illustrate the evolving lifecycle of adversarial threats. Ethical and regulatory considerations are examined, emphasizing responsible deployment and accountability. The cross-domain applicability of AML is discussed across various environments, including smart grids, autonomous systems, cloud, and edge computing. Real-world use cases are analyzed, with attention to adversarial datasets and evaluation practices. Additionally, the role of Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) systems is highlighted for enhancing interpretability and trust in AML-powered IDSs. This survey serves as a valuable resource for researchers and practitioners aiming at advancing AML in securing IoT ecosystems with innovative, ethical, and practical defense strategies. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).

[R0233] year=2026 | venue=Studies in Computational Intelligence | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Artificial Intelligence in Cyber Security: A Comprehensive Overview
KEYWORDS: Artificial Intelligence in Cybersecurity; Cyber Threat Detection; Ethical and Legal Challenges in AI Security; Generative AI; Machine Learning
ABSTRACT: The application of artificial intelligence (AI) in cybersecurity has revolutionized the way organizations respond to dynamic threats. AI technologies enable real-time analysis of large datasets to detect potential breaches and enhance response systems. The intelligence-based mechanism not only automates security processes but also enhances human capabilities, fostering greater synergy between AI and cybersecurity professionals. AI’s ability to continuously monitor user Behaviour and system vulnerabilities facilitates proactive risk management, emphasizing preventive over reactive measures. Given these advancements, the objective of this paper is to provide a comprehensive overview of AI in cybersecurity, examining its transformative impact while addressing the challenges associated with its implementation. By employing machine learning (ML), AI tools can learn from historical attack patterns, improving detection rates and ensuring defenses remain robust against emerging threats. However, despite its advantages, AI in cybersecurity presents challenges, including ethical concerns related to privacy, accountability, and potential misuse in cybercrime. These issues necessitate ongoing human oversight and the establishment of strong ethical frameworks. Through an analysis of AI’s ability to learn and adapt, this research underscores its critical role in combating sophisticated cyberattacks and strengthening the overall security landscape. Future work will prioritize autonomous intrusion detection, Behaviour-based analytics using deep learning, and Explainable AI (XAI) to enhance transparency and trust. The use of Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) Networks and transformer models will support proactive threat hunting, while federated and privacy-preserving AI will enable secure, regulation-compliant systems. To support this, the paper recommends clear AI testing standards, transparency requirements, ethical research incentives, and stronger public-private collaboration on AI auditing and bias mitigation. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.

[R0234] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings - 2025 Annual Computer Security Applications Conference Workshops, ACSACW 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: IntelForge: Multi-Agent LLM Framework for Cyber Threat Intelligence Enrichment
KEYWORDS: cyber threat intelligence; llms; multi-agent systems
ABSTRACT: Cyber threat intelligence (CTI) provides defenders with knowledge about attacks and adversaries, including their infrastructure, tools, and attack techniques. Enriching CTI with contextual information enables security teams to prioritize risks, derive actionable outputs, and respond to threats more effectively. Yet, the growing scale and complexity of cyberattacks make manual enrichment increasingly challenging, creating the need for automated and reliable solutions. In this paper, we present IntelForge, a novel multi-agent framework for automated CTI enrichment, built on orchestrated large language model (LLM)-based AI agents. Each agent in the system performs a distinct role, ranging from entity extraction to external retrieval, scoring, reporting, and evaluation, enabling a scalable and modular enrichment process. Leveraging task specialization and agent collaboration, IntelForge enriches raw CTI reports with high-value external sources and produces analyst-ready intelligence. To assess the quality of this enrichment, we compare IntelForge's source rankings to those of human experts and state-of-the-art LLM baselines. Our results show that IntelForge enriches CTI reports more effectively, achieving substantially lower deviation and higher correlation with human experts than single-LLM baselines. These findings demonstrate that structured agent-based LLM pipelines provide a powerful alternative to single-model solutions for CTI enrichment. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0235] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings - 2026 IEEE 50th Annual Computers, Software, and Applications Conference, COMPSAC 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Adaptive Agentic Honeypots: Decoupling Simulation from Reasoning for Robust Cyber Deception
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Cyber Deception; Honeypots; Large Language Models; SSH Security; State Management; Computational methods; Intelligent agents; Memory architecture; Multi agent systems; Network security; Agentic AI; Cybe deception; Decouplings; Deterministics; Honeypots; Interactivity; Language model; Large language model; SSH security; State management; Economic and social effects
ABSTRACT: Traditional honeypots lack the interactivity to engage skilled adversaries, while purely generative Large Language Model (LLM) honeypots suffer from non-deterministic hallucinations, state amnesia, and unrealistic latency signatures. To resolve this trade-off, this paper introduces Adaptive Agentic Honeypots (AAH), a hybrid cyber deception architecture that decouples deterministic system grounding from high-level intent reasoning. AAH integrates a Structured Simulation Layer (SSL) for routine interactions with a multi-agent LLM layer for complex reasoning, all governed by a centralized, ACID-compliant State and Memory Platform, and a strict policy validator. Evaluated against a MITRE ATT&CK-aligned kill-chain, AAH maintained adversary interaction 57% longer than static baselines (Cowrie) and doubled the engagement depth of LLM-only proxies. Crucially, AAH sustains a stealthy, dual-latency profile while eliminating hallucinated artifacts, demonstrating that state-first architectures effectively balance adaptive interactivity with operational safety and forensic reliability. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0236] year=2026 | venue=2026 6th International Conference on Computer Communication and Artificial Intelligence, CCAI 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: A Semantic-Consistent Privacy Middleware for MCP-driven Penetration Testing
KEYWORDS: Large Language Models; Model Context Protocol; Penetration Testing; Privacy Protection; Middleware; Network security; Privacy-preserving techniques; Sensitive data; Dynamic tools; Language model; Large language model; Model context protocol; Model contexts; Penetration testing; Privacy protection; Privacy risks; Third parties; User input; Semantics
ABSTRACT: The integration of LLMs via the Model Context Protocol (MCP) introduces privacy risks where intermediate tool outputs may expose sensitive assets. In sensitive scenarios such as penetration testing, critical information about attack chains and target assets can be leaked to third-party model services. Existing privacy methods mainly focus on filtering user inputs and fail to protect dynamic tool-generated context. To address this, we propose MCP Privacy Middleware (MCPPM), a lightweight privacy framework for the Local MCP + Remote LLM architecture. MCPPM is deployed at the local trust boundary as a bidirectional proxy to sanitize context streams. It combines hybrid NER (regex + domain-adapted Transformer) to identify security entities, an entity fusion mechanism to reduce semantic fragmentation, and a context-aware safe-zone strategy to selectively mask sensitive information while preserving reasoning semantics. Experiments on a cybersecurity dataset show that MCPPM achieves an F1 score of 0.903 and a semantic utility score of 0.921 with 101 ms average latency. Our results demonstrate that MCPPM enables secure deployment of MCP-based autonomous agents in zero-trust environments. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0237] year=2025 | venue=Eastern-European Journal of Enterprise Technologies | type=Article
TITLE: DESIGN AND DEVELOPMENT OF A LARGE LANGUAGE MODEL-BASED TOOL FOR VULNERABILITY DETECTION
KEYWORDS: artificial intelligence; large language models; multi-vector testing; vulnerability detection automation
ABSTRACT: The subject of this study is a tool for automating vulnerability detection using large language models, developed to reduce the time spent on conventional penetration testing. In addition, a detailed analysis has been conducted comparing the effectiveness of the automated approach with that of conventional manual security testing. The tool utilizes application programming interface access to LLMs, enabling the analysis of large volumes of data, the identification of complex relationships between system components, and the provision of interactive support to specialists during the testing process. By conducting experiments under actual conditions, the tool demonstrated the ability to integrate with popular penetration test tools and deal with real cyber threats, particularly in scenarios involving active attacks on networks and web applications. By automating routine tasks, such as configuration checks, analysis of tool outputs, and generating recommendations, the tool significantly reduces the workload on specialists. On average, the tool shortened the testing time by 54.4 % compared to a manual approach. Recall reached 94.7 % in network analysis scenarios but dropped to 66.7 % in web application testing, while the automated approach’s precision ranged from 80 % to 90 %. The study results confirmed that the application of large language models in the penetration testing process significantly reduces the time required to complete tasks and improves the accuracy of vulnerability detection. The tool could be used both independently and in combination with other automation tools, making it a versatile solution for organizations of various sizes. Thus, the proposed solution is a substantial contribution to the development of modern cybersecurity technologies and demonstrates the prospects of integrating artificial intelligence into automation processes Copyright © 2025 Authors. This is an open access article under the Creative Commons CC BY license

[R0238] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of 2025 2nd International Symposium on AI and Cybersecurity, ISAICS 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: DeepAttacker: Multi-Agents Collaboration based Breach and Attack Simulation
KEYWORDS: Large Language Model; Multi-Agent System; Offensive Security; Red Teaming; Computer crime; Intelligent agents; Life cycle; Network security; Agent collaboration; Cyber-attacks; Information infrastructures; Language model; Large language model; Multi agent; Multiagent systems (MASs); Offensive security; Red teaming; Security level; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: With the rapid increase of advanced persistent threats in critical information infrastructure, it's vital to evaluate the security level and spot the possible vulnerabilities ahead to avoid severe consequences of cyber-attacks. However, the complexity of APT made it difficult to simulate real life red teaming. In this paper, we present DeepAttacker, an end-to-end cooperative multi-agent collaboration based red teaming system featuring life-cycle attacking simulation. By employing multi-agent collaboration, the attack simulation process can be more intelligent, covering more attack actions and finalizing efficient attack chains, helps uncover deeply concealed vulnerabilities. Comprehensive experiments indicated DeepAttacker has a broader attack action space and higher rate of exploitation rate compared with other frameworks, achieved 5 x attack action coverage, which makes it suitable for application in simulating real-life APT attacks and spotting deeply concealed vulnerabilities. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0239] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings - 2025 IEEE 10th International Conference on Data Science in Cyberspace, DSC 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Enhancing Software Vulnerability Analysis via Large Language Models: A Systematic Review
KEYWORDS: large language models; penetration testing; software security analysis; static analysis integration; vulnerability detection; Application programs; Artificial intelligence; Binary codes; Computational methods; Model checking; Security systems; Static analysis; Language model; Large language model; Penetration testing; Security analysis; Software security; Software security analyze; Static analyze integration; Systematic Review; Vulnerability analysis; Vulnerability detection; Integration testing
ABSTRACT: In the current era of AI advancement, large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable breakthroughs in natural language understanding and code generation, and have been rapidly adopted in the field of software security analysis. This paper presents a systematic review of the research advancements in applying LLMs to vulnerability analysis tasks such as binary code analysis, static and dynamic vulnerability detection, and vulnerability validation. Studies show that the strong contextual understanding capabilities of LLMs enable the identification of common vulnerability types across multiple programming languages. Moreover, LLMs can act as intelligent attack agents to perform end-to-end penetration testing. The integration of LLMs with traditional static analysis techniques - such as symbolic execution and taint analysis - also demonstrates significant potential for improving the accuracy of security analysis. However, the application of LLMs in software security still faces several challenges, including high false positive rates, severe hallucination issues, limited context modeling capacity, and substantial resource consumption. This survey aims to provide researchers with a comprehensive technical overview, summarizing the latest achievements in leveraging LLMs for software security analysis, discussing their strengths and limitations, and outlining future research directions. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0240] year=2026 | venue=2026 5th International Conference on Electrical, Computer and Telecommunication Engineering, ICECTE 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Multi-Agent Phishing Detection And Deletion via Small VLM and LLM Reasoning
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Autonomous Cybersecurity; Email Security; Large Language Models (LLM); Multi-Agent Systems; Phishing Detection; Reasoning Models; Vision-Language Models (VLM); Autonomous agents; Computational linguistics; Computer crime; Computer hardware description languages; Crime; Cybersecurity; Electronic mail; Intelligent agents; Network security; Phishing; Remediation; Semantics; Visual languages; Agentic AI; Autonomous cybersecurity; Cyber security; E-mails security; Language model; Large language model; Multiagent systems (MASs); Phishing detections; Reasoning models; Vision-language model; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Phishing attacks remain one of the most pervasive cybersecurity threats, with email-based phishing responsible for over 90% of global data breaches. Traditional text-based detection systems achieve moderate success in identifying linguistic cues but fail to capture visual deception strategies such as brand impersonation, domain typosquatting, and counterfeit login layouts. This paper introduces an autonomous multi-agent framework that leverages reasoning capable Vision-Language Models (VLMs) for end-to-end phishing detection and remediation within Gmail environments. The proposed agent continuously monitors spam folders, analyzes rendered email screenshots, and autonomously executes remediation actions permanently deleting confirmed phishing messages (confidence ≥ 0.80) or restoring legitimate ones to the inbox. Experiments evaluate reasoning VLMs (Qwen2-VL-2B, Qwen2.5-VL-7B) against standard architectures (LLaVA-1.6, Moondream2, Phi-3.5-Vision) across 150 real-world phishing samples. Results show that reasoning-enhanced VLMs achieve 82-85% detection accuracy, reducing false positives by approximately 15% compared to standard VLMs (58-63% accuracy). Furthermore, integrating a lightweight reasoning LLM (Qwen-3-4B-Thinking) improves multimodal consistency, yielding an additional 3% accuracy gain and greater semantic alignment between visual and textual cues. This study advances the field of agentic AI for cybersecurity by demonstrating the practicality of fully autonomous, multimodal phishing email management on commodity hardware. The complete implementation publicly available at: https://github.com/rafi79/AI-Phishing-Detector. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0241] year=2025 | venue=Computers, Materials and Continua | type=Article
TITLE: Utilizing Fine-Tuning of Large Language Models for Generating Synthetic Payloads: Enhancing Web Application Cybersecurity through Innovative Penetration Testing Techniques
